                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_threat_right:  `<strong>That's the threat.</strong> The CEO attacked a still-provisional leadership identity, not the slides. Reading it as identity work — not a roadmap-quality problem — is the only way to respond to the right thing.`,
                    coach_r1_threat_task:   `<strong>You're hiding in the spreadsheet.</strong> You've rebuilt a beautiful roadmap, but the pulse survey is still at 38% and a second lead is wavering. The CEO didn't say your slides were wrong — what identity question are you avoiding?`,
                    coach_r1_threat_off:    `<strong>That's a deflection.</strong> Politics and competence are real, but the interruption landed on Roche's unresolved question — am I a leader, or an impostor? Name <em>that</em> threat before you act.`,
                    coach_r1_own:           `<strong>Owning the miss is right</strong> — but you're still calling this a slide problem. The acknowledgement only does identity work if you also name that a leader, not a roadmap, is what was questioned.`,
                    coach_r1_own_sharp:     `<strong>Sharp.</strong> You named the identity threat <em>and</em> chose to own the miss. Acknowledging it without collapsing signals a leader who can hold a setback — credibility and confidence both move up.`,
                    coach_r1_defend:        `<strong>Defending hardens the room against you.</strong> Arguing the CEO misread it tells the committee Roche can't take a hit — exactly the doubt they already hold. Credibility drops.`,
                    coach_r1_deflect:       `<strong>Deflection is read instantly.</strong> Blaming the merger and the timeline reads as a leader dodging accountability. The two wavering leads see it and lean further toward the door.`,
                    coach_r1_withdraw:      `<strong>Withdrawal is the spiral.</strong> Going quiet and re-reading R&D detail is clutching the proven old self exactly when a new one is demanded. A second lead resigns — and Roche is now leading from absence.`,
                    coach_r1_lowcandor:     `<strong>You're naming a slide problem to avoid an identity problem.</strong> Low willingness to do the identity work means the 38% never moves — you'll fix the roadmap and leave the self untouched.`,
                    coach_r2_solo:          `<strong>Isolation is the real threat.</strong> White-knuckling this solo to prove she's strong enough means decisions at midnight with no one to test them. Petriglieri's point: leaders are <em>held</em> into their identity. Who is holding Roche?`,
                    coach_r2_one:           `<strong>One genuine relationship</strong> is a real holding environment — better than eight shallow ones. Make sure it's someone who can actually contain the anxiety, not just advise.`,
                    coach_r2_two:           `<strong>One or two — exactly right.</strong> A focused holding environment lets Roche tolerate the anxiety of transition without acting it out. Depth beats breadth here.`,
                    coach_r2_sponsoronly:   `<strong>The sponsor pulls you backward.</strong> Powerful, yes — but leaning only on the person tied to your old expert identity makes it harder to let the provisional new self emerge. Pair them with a peer or coach.`,
                    coach_r2_ask_hold:      `<strong>The right ask.</strong> Asking to be <em>held</em> — a place to feel the anxiety without acting it out — is the developmental move. From a held place, Roche can decide clearly instead of reacting.`,
                    coach_r2_ask_advice:    `<strong>Advice helps the task, not the self.</strong> Tactical help on the roadmap is useful, but a holding environment contains anxiety — without that, you've found a consultant, not a holder.`,
                    coach_r2_ask_reassure:  `<strong>Reassurance is a sugar high.</strong> Being told “you're good enough” soothes for an hour and leaves the provisional self just as fragile. Ask to be held, not flattered.`,
                    coach_r2_ask_rescue:    `<strong>Rescue undermines the identity you're building.</strong> Having someone intervene on her behalf with the CEO tells the room Roche can't hold her own chair. It buys relief and spends credibility.`,
                    coach_r3_armour:        `<strong>The room sees through the armour.</strong> Roche declares everything's under control — but the two leads were in the committee meeting; they know it wasn't. Performed certainty reads as a lie and confidence ticks <em>down</em>.`,
                    coach_r3_collapse:      `<strong>Authenticity without a held stance becomes contagion.</strong> “I don't know if I'm the right person” is honest — but now 240 people are wondering the same thing. Where is the line between honesty and abdication?`,
                    coach_r3_held:          `<strong>Held authenticity.</strong> Owning the miss while holding a clear direction — vulnerability with a spine — is congruence between values, identity and action. The room can follow a leader who is honest <em>and</em> steady.`,
                    coach_r3_ownask_sharp:  `<strong>That opening line lands.</strong> Naming the miss and asking the two leads for help — from a held stance — treats them as partners, not problems. It's the move most likely to keep them and recover the division.`,
                    coach_r3_command:       `<strong>Commanding from a known-false position.</strong> “Everything is under control” to people who watched it fall apart costs trust you can't afford. A lead resigns; confidence drops to the low 30s.`,
                    coach_r3_confess:       `<strong>That's collapse, not candor.</strong> Telling the leads you don't know if you're right for the job hands them the exit and tells the division to doubt you too. Honesty needs a spine.`,
                    coach_r3_transactional: `<strong>A bribe isn't leadership.</strong> Trading a quiet six months for a future promotion treats a relationship as a transaction — and word travels. Credibility and sustainability both crater.`,
                    coach_r3_reveal_none:   `<strong>Zero disclosure rings hollow.</strong> Showing no doubt at all to a room that knows better isn't strength — it's armour with better posture. A measured, owned uncertainty would have been more credible.`,
                    coach_r3_reveal_all:    `<strong>Over-sharing spreads the spiral.</strong> Disclosing the sleepless nights and the whole impostor feeling makes the team carry Roche's anxiety. Reveal enough to be human, not enough to destabilise.`,
                    coach_r4_specific:      `<strong>A purpose you can lead by.</strong> Specific, honest, and rooted in who Roche actually is — it tells her what to do at the next interruption and would survive a second public setback. This is the difference between a self and a slogan.`,
                    coach_r4_slogan:        `<strong>That would survive zero setbacks.</strong> “Inspiring, innovative leader” gives Roche nothing to do when a CEO interrupts her again. Make it specific enough to lead by and honest enough to hold.`,
                    coach_r4_expert:        `<strong>That's the old identity in a new title.</strong> “Smartest person in the room” is the expert self the promotion outgrew. It's exactly the identity the CEO said wasn't leadership.`,
                    coach_r4_results:       `<strong>A job-preservation goal, not a purpose.</strong> Hitting the committee's numbers to keep her job gives Roche no compass under pressure — the next setback will read as another referendum on whether she belongs.`,
                    coach_r4_bridge:        `<strong>Reframing as a business plan rebuilds credibility</strong> — linking the science to market outcomes is what the CEO asked for. Anchor it to a purpose and it becomes a leadership stance, not just better slides.`,
                    coach_r4_bridge_sharp:  `<strong>Purpose and plan together.</strong> A specific leadership purpose <em>and</em> a roadmap that bridges science to outcomes — the committee sees a leader, not a scientist with slides. The three held promotions go ahead.`,
                    coach_r4_polish:        `<strong>Better slides, same problem.</strong> Polishing the R&D plan with an executive summary leaves the substance unchanged. The CEO's objection wasn't formatting — it was that you read like a scientist.`,
                    coach_r4_capitulate:    `<strong>You over-corrected.</strong> Dropping the science ambition to look commercial abandons the very value Roche was promoted to unlock. That's not becoming a leader — it's erasing yourself to please the room.`,
                    coach_r4_grand:         `<strong>A grand relaunch is theatre.</strong> One big event resets the narrative for a day; confidence rebuilds through visible follow-through, not a show. Sequence wins the leads can actually see.`,
                    coach_r4_quiet:         `<strong>Silence isn't a plan.</strong> Letting results speak with no communicated recovery path leaves an anxious division to fill the gap with doubt. Name the commitments and follow through.`,

